-- title:
--   Extension of places with residue retraction along finite extensions
-- statement:
--   Let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field, $\Omega$ a field equipped with a $\kappa$-algebra structure, and $V$ a place of $\Omega$ over $\kappa$ in the sense of the project, i.e. a valuation subring $\mathcal O_V = V.\mathrm{toValuationSubring}$ of $\Omega$ which contains the image of $\kappa$, is not all of $\Omega$, and is a principal ideal ring. Let $\mathrm{res}$ be a ring homomorphism from the residue field of $\mathcal O_V$ to $\kappa$ which is a retraction of the structure map, that is $\mathrm{res}(\iota(c)) = c$ for every $c \in \kappa$, where $\iota$ denotes the algebra map $\kappa \to \mathrm{ResidueField}(\mathcal O_V)$. Assume there is an element $x \in \Omega$ transcendental over $\kappa$ with $\Omega$ finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $\kappa(x)$ generated by $x$. Let $\Omega_1$ be a field which is simultaneously a $\kappa$-algebra and an $\Omega$-algebra, compatibly as a scalar tower, and finite as an $\Omega$-module. Then there exist a place $V_1$ of $\Omega_1$ over $\kappa$, a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{res}_1$ from the residue field of $\mathcal O_{V_1}$ to $\kappa$, and a ring homomorphism $\iota_{\mathcal O} : \mathcal O_V \to \mathcal O_{V_1}$ such that: the preimage of $\mathcal O_{V_1}$ under the algebra map $\Omega \to \Omega_1$ is exactly $\mathcal O_V$; $\iota_{\mathcal O}$ is induced by $\Omega \to \Omega_1$ on underlying elements; $\mathrm{res}_1$ is again a retraction of the structure map of $\kappa$; and $\mathrm{res}_1 \circ \mathrm{residue}_{\mathcal O_{V_1}} \circ \iota_{\mathcal O} = \mathrm{res} \circ \mathrm{residue}_{\mathcal O_V}$ on $\mathcal O_V$.
--
--   This is the classical statement that a place of a one-variable function field over an algebraically closed field extends to any finite extension of the field, the extension lying over the given place, together with the fact that all places involved are $\kappa$-rational so that the chosen residue retraction can be propagated compatibly. It is used in the analysis of reductions of modular curves, where points of a curve over $\kappa$ are tracked through finite extensions of function fields via places and their residue maps.
